    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s42949-026-00388-z",
    "abstract": "Urban weather and climate modeling is challenged by the highly heterogeneous and dynamic nature of cities. It exhibits a persistent trilemma between spatial granularity, spatiotemporal coverage, and physical interpretability. We articulate this challenge and propose a hybrid framework integrating physics-based models, urban observations, and machine learning. Framing this challenge as an integration problem across methods and scales, we provide a structured guide for next-generation, decision-relevant urban weather and climate modeling.",
    "field": "Urban weather and climate modeling",
    "problem": "Urban weather and climate modeling faces a persistent trilemma where existing methods must trade off between achieving high spatial granularity, broad spatiotemporal coverage, and physical interpretability, as no single approach can satisfy all three attributes simultaneously.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a hybrid framework that integrates physics-based models, urban observations, and machine learning to resolve the trilemma between spatial granularity, spatiotemporal coverage, and physical interpretability in urban weather and climate modeling.",
    "task": "The task is to address the trilemma between spatial granularity, coverage, and interpretability in urban weather and climate modeling by proposing a hybrid framework that integrates physics-based models, urban observations, and machine learning.",
